WEBVTT

1
00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:13.800
One tenant per client. Open a customer and everything is scoped to them.

2
00:00:13.800 --> 00:00:19.600
The week ahead. Scheduled tickets and maintenance falling due, merged into one list.

3
00:00:19.600 --> 00:00:23.600
Monitor status and the client's sites sit underneath, as reference rather than headline.

4
00:00:23.600 --> 00:00:29.600
The customer record. Contacts, addresses, sites, and shortcuts into their documentation.

5
00:00:29.600 --> 00:00:35.600
A service desk you own, not one you rent. Every client in one queue.

6
00:00:35.600 --> 00:00:43.900
A ticket that already knows the customer — active contract, hours used, and the SLA countdown.

7
00:00:43.900 --> 00:00:49.700
Internal notes stay internal. Client-visible replies are kept visibly separate.

8
00:00:49.700 --> 00:00:55.700
The credentials this job needs, by title and username only. Secrets are never rendered here.

9
00:00:55.700 --> 00:01:01.700
The runbook lives beside the work, linked from the ticket that needed it.

10
00:01:01.700 --> 00:01:06.700
Per-client documentation and a shared knowledge base, searchable across titles and body text.

11
00:01:06.700 --> 00:01:12.700
Light or dark, per person. Twelve themes ship, and every screen honours the one you pick.

12
00:01:12.700 --> 00:01:20.500
The asset record: identity, network, OS, hardware, rack position, warranty and lifecycle.

13
00:01:20.500 --> 00:01:24.500
Lifecycle and warranty dates mean the refresh conversation happens before the hardware fails.

14
00:01:24.500 --> 00:01:31.000
What is physically in the comms room — every device at its U position, linked to its asset record.

15
00:01:31.000 --> 00:01:36.500
Address space per client: subnets with VLAN, gateway and DNS, and which addresses are free.

16
00:01:36.500 --> 00:01:42.500
The vault: AES-GCM encrypted at rest, per-entry access rules, audited reveals.

17
00:01:42.500 --> 00:01:49.000
Dispatch the week. Drag a ticket onto a technician's row; SLA risk surfaces at the top.

18
00:01:49.000 --> 00:01:54.500
Recurring maintenance runs on its own schedule, with per-task sign-off.

19
00:01:54.500 --> 00:02:02.800
Projects with real dependencies. Drag a bar to reschedule; blocked work says so, and by what.

20
00:02:02.800 --> 00:02:06.800
The dependency table underneath is where the blocking relationships are edited.

21
00:02:06.800 --> 00:02:11.800
Back to light for the commercial side. Draft, sent and accepted quotes across every client.

22
00:02:11.800 --> 00:02:18.300
Quote to signature: a PDF, an email, and a signing link the customer uses without an account.

23
00:02:18.300 --> 00:02:24.800
Every invoice line carries its origin, so a billing query is answerable without reconstructing it.

24
00:02:24.800 --> 00:02:30.300
Agreements track themselves: block hours with rollover, auto-renewal, and a profitability snapshot.

25
00:02:30.300 --> 00:02:36.800
Revenue against cost of delivery, per client, drawn from logged time and issued invoices.

26
00:02:36.800 --> 00:02:42.800
Client health combines SLA performance, ticket velocity, billing aging and engagement.

27
00:02:42.800 --> 00:02:48.800
And the whole book of business: every tenant, every site, on one page.

28
00:02:48.800 --> 00:02:54.300
Modules are opt-in. The service desk, AI assistance and the rest stay off until you turn them on.
